1
00:00:00,760 --> 00:00:08,442
Hello, I’m Sunshine from 3S Lab. Our project is Reactive PegPulse. One quote. Three chains.

2
00:00:08,442 --> 00:00:09,962
Anyone can verify.

3
00:00:10,292 --> 00:00:16,481
Imagine volunteering for a community treasury. One USDC buys only 0.9930 USDT.

4
00:00:16,481 --> 00:00:19,179
Would you ask others to review it?

5
00:00:19,509 --> 00:00:22,186
First, check that the alert reached the chain.

6
00:00:22,186 --> 00:00:24,921
Then ask whether market evidence is sufficient.

7
00:00:24,921 --> 00:00:28,004
Anyone with a mobile browser can follow the receipts.

8
00:00:28,764 --> 00:00:35,734
The center line is parity. The green band extends half a percent on either side.

9
00:00:36,064 --> 00:00:40,672
The control quote, 0.9990, is ten basis points below parity.

10
00:00:40,672 --> 00:00:45,972
The animation’s 0.9950 sits exactly on the boundary. Both stay quiet.

11
00:00:46,302 --> 00:00:49,711
At 0.9930, the gap reaches seventy basis points.

12
00:00:49,711 --> 00:00:54,044
Crossing the threshold makes the contract request a callback.

13
00:00:54,804 --> 00:00:59,153
These are real testnet transactions. I published a simulated quote on Ethereum Sepolia.

14
00:00:59,153 --> 00:01:02,302
The Lasna contract subscribes to that publisher’s quote events.

15
00:01:02,632 --> 00:01:04,489
Reactive calls react() on Lasna.

16
00:01:04,489 --> 00:01:09,306
The contract checks the source, threshold and duplicates, then requests a callback.

17
00:01:09,306 --> 00:01:11,918
Base Sepolia records the alert automatically.

18
00:01:12,248 --> 00:01:17,374
To match the event, compare the source transaction hash and log index two.

19
00:01:17,374 --> 00:01:21,668
The three UTC block times are 13:38:12, 13:38:12 and 13:38:14.

20
00:01:21,998 --> 00:01:26,263
The first and last block timestamps differ by two seconds in this run.

21
00:01:26,263 --> 00:01:28,640
Each receipt has its own explorer link.

22
00:01:29,400 --> 00:01:32,016
An off-chain model prioritizes review.

23
00:01:32,016 --> 00:01:38,556
It needs earlier pair history and contemporaneous independent dollar references for both coins.

24
00:01:38,886 --> 00:01:45,119
The public run had no dollar references. The alert was retained and the score withheld.

25
00:01:45,119 --> 00:01:47,196
That is the observed outcome.

26
00:01:47,526 --> 00:01:53,783
For the two alternatives I keep the on-chain alert fixed and switch labeled synthetic evidence.

27
00:01:53,783 --> 00:01:59,580
Corroborating dollar weakness escalates review. Near-par references support observation.

28
00:02:00,340 --> 00:02:04,377
The tool records alerts and moves zero stablecoin principal.

29
00:02:04,377 --> 00:02:09,559
Repeating a source log sends one callback. Redelivering it records one alert.

30
00:02:09,889 --> 00:02:13,808
The newer contracts cap both individual and lifetime service payments.

31
00:02:13,808 --> 00:02:18,343
An empty balance or budget stops new work. Top-ups do not reset the lifetime cap.

32
00:02:18,673 --> 00:02:24,047
All fifteen local tests passed, including valid runs, rejection, replay and recovery.

33
00:02:24,047 --> 00:02:27,904
The public receipts shown earlier predate the added fee caps.

34
00:02:28,664 --> 00:02:34,205
Community volunteers can read all three receipts without connecting a wallet or paying a fee.

35
00:02:34,205 --> 00:02:38,435
Bilingual captions and a transcript let people check at their own pace.

36
00:02:38,765 --> 00:02:44,868
Our design goals are wider participation and transparent accountability, aligned with SDGs 10 and 16.

37
00:02:44,868 --> 00:02:49,280
Next we will test how many community members can verify an alert unaided.

38
00:02:50,760 --> 00:02:52,718
This is a prerecorded simulated Q&A.

39
00:02:52,718 --> 00:02:56,471
First question: how can a judge verify that the destination executed?

40
00:02:56,801 --> 00:03:02,417
Open the Base Sepolia receipt and find the recipient’s PriceDeviation event.

41
00:03:02,417 --> 00:03:08,107
Match its source transaction hash and log index two to the event ID on Lasna.

42
00:03:08,437 --> 00:03:10,525
This verifies that the alert was recorded.

43
00:03:10,525 --> 00:03:14,502
Two seconds is the difference between the endpoint block timestamps in this run.

44
00:03:15,262 --> 00:03:20,061
Second question: if we have price oracles, what does Reactive add?

45
00:03:20,391 --> 00:03:22,819
Price oracles can supply dollar references.

46
00:03:22,819 --> 00:03:29,367
Our contract subscribes to a specific quote event and requests a cross-chain callback when the threshold is crossed.

47
00:03:29,367 --> 00:03:31,400
Anyone can match the three receipts.

48
00:03:31,730 --> 00:03:36,597
The simulated quote triggered a real callback. Live oracle feeds are a next step.

49
00:03:36,597 --> 00:03:41,586
Without dollar references, the review tool retained the alert and withheld a score.

50
00:03:42,346 --> 00:03:48,840
Third question: when volunteers see one pool quote fall, can they conclude that a stablecoin lost its peg?

51
00:03:49,170 --> 00:03:52,045
One pool quote warrants investigation.

52
00:03:52,045 --> 00:03:57,642
The model also needs history and independent dollar prices for both coins.

53
00:03:57,642 --> 00:04:02,484
The public run kept the alert and identified the missing inputs.

54
00:04:02,814 --> 00:04:11,700
Volunteers can flag the missing prices and use the same alert with two synthetic inputs to check when the model escalates review.

55
00:04:12,460 --> 00:04:17,519
Final question: what if a callback fails or an event arrives twice?

56
00:04:17,848 --> 00:04:21,940
A failed call leaves the destination count unchanged.

57
00:04:21,940 --> 00:04:27,190
Inspect the proxy, target event, balance, debt and remaining budget.

58
00:04:27,190 --> 00:04:30,974
Authorized redelivery can record that event once.

59
00:04:31,304 --> 00:04:34,974
You can rerun all fifteen tests from the repository.

60
00:04:34,974 --> 00:04:39,280
Please collect additional questions for my written follow-up.
